{"name":"Cardio / sweating","deep":true,"image":null,"mentions":19,"casual":{"lead":["Users do cardio or sweat in heat to shed fluid and look leaner and less puffy, particularly in the face.","Sweat is a watery fluid released by eccrine glands to cool the body, with roughly 0.2 to 1 percent dissolved solutes including sodium. During average-intensity exercise, water losses can reach about 2 litres per hour, and adults can sweat 2 to 4 litres per hour at peak. The sources reviewed describe how sweat works and do not address facial appearance."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People add running, cycling or other cardio, sometimes in warm conditions or with extra layers, and drink fluids afterward. The leaner look is described as short-term and returns to baseline as fluids are replaced."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Heavy sweating can cause dehydration and electrolyte loss, and sodium depletion is associated with cramps. Heat adds risk when fluid is not replaced."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[],"links":{"studies":[{"title":"Rationale and Design of CARDIO-TTRansform, a Phase 3 Trial of Eplontersen in Transthyretin Amyloid Cardiomyopathy.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42104840/","source":"Circulation. Heart failure","year":2026,"cites":null,"kind":"Randomized Controlled Trial"},{"title":"Effect of glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists and co-agonists on body composition: Systematic review and network meta-analysis.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39719170/","source":"Metabolism: clinical and experimental","year":2025,"cites":null,"kind":"Network Meta-Analysis"},{"title":"Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors for premenstrual syndrome and premenstrual dysphoric disorder.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39140320/","source":"The Cochrane database of systematic reviews","year":2024,"cites":null,"kind":"Systematic Review"},{"title":"Sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 inhibitors (SGLT2) in frail or older people with